1
00:00:19,686 --> 00:00:20,820
[engine revs]

2
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:28,299
[Jerry] This is 1957 BMW 507 Roadster,

3
00:00:29,099 --> 00:00:33,100
in papyrus white with blue leather
interior and the optional hard top. 

4
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:36,966
The body is almost
entirely hand formed of aluminum,

5
00:00:37,066 --> 00:00:38,766
and no two are exactly alike.

6
00:00:39,199 --> 00:00:43,633
So the hard top on this car
wouldn’t fit on any other BMW 507.

7
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,433
It’s got a 3.2 liter all aluminum V8

8
00:00:48,666 --> 00:00:50,566
that makes 150 horsepower,

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,466
and a four speed gear box,
front disc brakes,

10
00:00:53,566 --> 00:00:56,333
with a top speed of 136 miles an hour.

11
00:00:57,833 --> 00:01:00,533
This is something that happens
once in a while in the car world,

12
00:01:00,599 --> 00:01:02,900
where a car company
gets a little out of control,

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,633
a little excited about making something
better than they ever have before,

14
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,633
and they end up putting too much into it,

15
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:10,899
too much quality, too much detail.

16
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:15,266
and they suddenly realize, "There’s no way
we can make a profit on this."

17
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:18,033
They made about 252 of these

18
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:20,366
before they came to their senses and went,

19
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:22,966
"We are losing our ass on this car. 

20
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:26,633
Stop! Stop making them!"
And that’s what’s so wonderful about it.

21
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:29,966
Here’s a car that shouldn’t even exist,
and yet, here it is.

22
00:01:30,866 --> 00:01:35,633
The car was designed by a German
industrial designer, Albrecht von Goertz.

23
00:01:35,699 --> 00:01:38,366
He seems open to compromise
with a room full of accountants.

24
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:40,000
The Germans are very interesting.

25
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:42,600
Sometimes they make things
very serious and heavy-handed,

26
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:46,033
and then sometimes they’re able to make
things like this, very light and delicate.

27
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,199
That combination of talents
can be very potent automotively, 

28
00:01:50,243 --> 00:01:53,713
and also in the acting profession,
which is why it is the perfect car...

29
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:54,647
Let’s go for a ride.

30
00:01:54,948 --> 00:01:58,718
...for my very gifted, very artistic,
and very precise special guest...

31
00:01:58,885 --> 00:02:01,654
Oh my, God, you’re so perfectly dressed
for the car.

32
00:02:01,821 --> 00:02:03,423
...Mr. Christoph Waltz.

33
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:06,966
Even though Christoph
is mostly a dramatic actor,

34
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:08,666
he’s so good at what he does,

35
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:11,533
there’s almost a humor
that he brings to his performances

36
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:14,633
particularly in Quentin Tarantino’s
masterpiece,

37
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:16,333
Inglourious Basterds,

38
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:20,933
where he plays the most
gleefully possible SS Nazi officer.

39
00:02:21,074 --> 00:02:22,675
[in French]

40
00:02:22,766 --> 00:02:25,166
[Jerry] I’m Jewish, but I love this Nazi.

41
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:26,666
-Ooh...
-[phone rings]

42
00:02:26,779 --> 00:02:30,049
-Hi, Christoph, it’s Jerry Seinfeld.
-[Christoph] Oh, hello. How are ya?

43
00:02:30,116 --> 00:02:33,419
Right now,
I’m at the Busy Bee Hardware store.

44
00:02:33,799 --> 00:02:37,500
[Jerry] Oh yeah, I know where that is.
I also did a movie about bees.

45
00:02:37,566 --> 00:02:40,700
-Were they busy?
-Christoph, there’s no lazy bees.

46
00:02:42,666 --> 00:02:47,400
I’m Jerry Seinfeld and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

47
00:02:53,366 --> 00:02:54,700
-Hey, Christoph.
-Good morning.

48
00:02:54,799 --> 00:02:57,299
I’m trying
to find something in centimeters.

49
00:02:57,466 --> 00:03:00,100
-Why?
-Why? Because inches don't make sense.

50
00:03:01,748 --> 00:03:02,849
[Jerry] Here in America,

51
00:03:02,966 --> 00:03:05,766
we tried to switch over
to the metric system at one point

52
00:03:05,952 --> 00:03:07,921
but it was hard and
we just got really tired.

53
00:03:07,987 --> 00:03:08,922
-It is tiring.
-Yes.

54
00:03:10,123 --> 00:03:12,158
[inaudible]

55
00:03:12,299 --> 00:03:13,899
[Jerry] You love mechanical things.

56
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:15,966
I think we did talk one time,

57
00:03:16,033 --> 00:03:18,333
about the fountain pen scene.

58
00:03:18,399 --> 00:03:22,833
When under new management, there’s
always a slight duplication of efforts.

59
00:03:24,137 --> 00:03:26,272
Most of it being a complete waste of time,

60
00:03:26,399 --> 00:03:28,266
but needs to be done nevertheless.

61
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:31,566
Did it ever not go well
when you were shooting it?

62
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:32,466
Yeah.

63
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:33,933
[blows raspberry]

64
00:03:39,352 --> 00:03:40,987
[Christoph] It's just lovely.

65
00:03:41,166 --> 00:03:42,799
You picked exactly the right thing.

66
00:03:42,966 --> 00:03:45,399
You know,
I remember this car from my childhood.

67
00:03:45,466 --> 00:03:47,333
[Jerry] Really? In Austria?

68
00:03:47,933 --> 00:03:51,299
[Christoph]
This I can spend the morning with.

69
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:52,600
Never mind the engine.

70
00:03:52,666 --> 00:03:55,566
-Yeah.
-But this, this is so beautiful.

71
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:58,833
Christoph, you’re forgetting
one of the essential parts of this show.

72
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:02,133
You have to get in the car.

73
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:12,452
This is such a wonderful speed...

74
00:04:12,518 --> 00:04:14,454
-Uh-huh.
-...to be cruising along.

75
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:16,533
-It’s so civilized.
-Yes.

76
00:04:16,623 --> 00:04:18,124
[Jerry] Had coffee yet this morning?

77
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:19,759
-[Christoph] No.
-[Jerry] You like coffee?

78
00:04:19,826 --> 00:04:21,661
-[Christoph] I love coffee.
-[Jerry] Great. Me, too.

79
00:04:24,466 --> 00:04:26,866
-So you’re from Vienna, Austria.
-Yeah.

80
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:29,566
What’s the difference between
Austrians and Germans?

81
00:04:29,733 --> 00:04:34,866
What’s the difference between
dancing a waltz and the goose step?

82
00:04:40,599 --> 00:04:42,866
So have you seen any episodes
of this show?

83
00:04:43,033 --> 00:04:46,900
No, I’ve purposely tried to
keep myself innocent.

84
00:04:46,966 --> 00:04:47,800
Okay.

85
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:49,566
This must all seem very odd.

86
00:04:49,666 --> 00:04:52,366
Well, I understand the basis of this,

87
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:54,500
which doesn’t make it any less odd.

88
00:04:57,966 --> 00:04:59,433
[woman] You look so dapper!

89
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:01,333
Thank you.

90
00:05:01,401 --> 00:05:03,036
-[man] Comedians in Cars?
-Yes, sir.

91
00:05:03,166 --> 00:05:04,966
-[man] Great show!
-Thank you, appreciate it.

92
00:05:05,038 --> 00:05:06,372
He’s never even seen it.

93
00:05:06,506 --> 00:05:08,775
Oh! You gotta watch every episode
and catch up!

94
00:05:08,866 --> 00:05:09,766
Have a nice day.

95
00:05:10,233 --> 00:05:12,533
See? You’re on a huge hit show right now.

96
00:05:12,866 --> 00:05:15,366
Make no mistake, I'm fully aware of that.

97
00:05:19,099 --> 00:05:22,633
[Jerry] Now when you played Hans Landa
in Inglourious Basterds,

98
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:25,866
did you think
there’s something funny about this guy?

99
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,166
He was the most evil character
in the story

100
00:05:31,599 --> 00:05:33,833
but he was having the best time.

101
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:37,599
And also, he left enough space

102
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:40,233
for the spectator to make up his mind.

103
00:05:40,707 --> 00:05:46,946
Do I see the funny element or do I see
the superficial horror?

104
00:05:47,133 --> 00:05:49,966
[Jerry] Did you have any hesitation
about doing that part?

105
00:05:50,033 --> 00:05:50,900
[Christoph] No.

106
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,033
You can imagine how many Nazi’s
I’ve been offered in my career.

107
00:05:56,166 --> 00:05:58,233
And I never did a single one.

108
00:05:58,300 --> 00:05:59,700
-Oh, really?
-Never.

109
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:02,633
Because this character was so complex...

110
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:04,533
-Right.
-...and so interesting.

111
00:06:04,666 --> 00:06:08,033
[Jerry] I like how good villains
are always very well-mannered.

112
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:10,033
-The elegance.
-I totally agree.

113
00:06:10,099 --> 00:06:12,400
And you brought a lot of that
to that character.

114
00:06:12,472 --> 00:06:18,211
[Christoph] That’s something in comedians
that I admire, the ease and lightness

115
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:21,933
with which some of them
can address very intense topics.

116
00:06:22,066 --> 00:06:27,366
Apparently, even,
even crows have a sense of humor.

117
00:06:27,466 --> 00:06:28,966
-Crows do?
-Crows do.

118
00:06:29,066 --> 00:06:31,733
They trick each other
just for the fun of it.

119
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:33,166
Monkeys do it to.

120
00:06:33,466 --> 00:06:35,500
-Monkeys, I expect that of monkeys.
-Yeah.

121
00:06:39,533 --> 00:06:42,766
[Jerry] We’re going to Torrance,
which is just another couple of exits.

122
00:06:42,869 --> 00:06:47,607
My thought here was that because of your 
very eclectic background of nationalities

123
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:50,977
there was only one place
that could possibly service

124
00:06:51,043 --> 00:06:54,480
all of these difference facets
of your background.

125
00:06:57,166 --> 00:06:59,700
The International House of Pancakes.

126
00:07:00,019 --> 00:07:02,422
[operatic instrumental music playing]

127
00:07:16,799 --> 00:07:18,466
[waitress] Anything to drink?

128
00:07:18,566 --> 00:07:20,000
I’d like a cup of coffee.

129
00:07:28,299 --> 00:07:33,033
I have to show you pictures
of the cafes that I grew up in.

130
00:07:33,133 --> 00:07:34,400
Are they like this?

131
00:07:35,121 --> 00:07:36,289
[chattering]

132
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:39,366
Not really.

133
00:07:39,433 --> 00:07:40,633
[chuckles]

134
00:07:44,666 --> 00:07:46,299
Are you careful about what you eat?

135
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:47,833
Would you eat that?

136
00:07:54,233 --> 00:07:57,533
[Christoph] No wonder that health care
is so expensive.

137
00:07:57,633 --> 00:08:00,400
[Jerry] We have to fix all of that
after you eat it.

138
00:08:00,479 --> 00:08:04,050
Don’t worry about the consequences.
You eat what you want, we’ll fix it.

139
00:08:04,299 --> 00:08:06,000
That’s the American way of life.

140
00:08:07,766 --> 00:08:09,333
I don’t know why you’re living here.

141
00:08:12,391 --> 00:08:14,460
I think its a very practical thing.

142
00:08:14,527 --> 00:08:16,529
Its more of a craft than an art.

143
00:08:16,629 --> 00:08:17,997
The art is in the writing.

144
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:20,966
I can't agree with that,
I think they are both arts.

145
00:08:21,166 --> 00:08:23,933
I've heard Quentin Tarantino say
if he didn't find you,

146
00:08:24,033 --> 00:08:25,766
he could not have made the movie.

147
00:08:25,866 --> 00:08:28,633
I don't believe that.
There are good actors and bad actors.

148
00:08:28,674 --> 00:08:32,979
I think there's appropriate casting
and wrong casting.

149
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:36,233
So, if you're right for the part,
you will be good.

150
00:08:36,333 --> 00:08:39,466
And, if your wrong,
you can be the best actor in the world,

151
00:08:39,566 --> 00:08:40,666
and you will be bad.

152
00:08:40,766 --> 00:08:42,333
The rest is, is...

153
00:08:42,866 --> 00:08:43,733
showing off.

154
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:51,533
What is Vienna known for?

155
00:08:51,766 --> 00:08:53,866
Music, architecture.

156
00:08:53,966 --> 00:08:57,837
You know, on the list of cities
with the best quality of life,

157
00:08:58,333 --> 00:09:01,400
Vienna is either number one or number two.

158
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,566
What other city would be up there?

159
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:06,900
Torrance?

160
00:09:13,466 --> 00:09:17,133
What are the similarities between
Vienna and Torrance?

161
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:20,166
There is an aspect of hopelessness
in both.

162
00:09:23,733 --> 00:09:26,033
Is anybody funny in Vienna?

163
00:09:26,100 --> 00:09:27,100
Oh yeah.

164
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:30,533
The comedy scene in Vienna
is very authentic.

165
00:09:30,633 --> 00:09:33,066
Can you give me an example
of a Viennese joke?

166
00:09:33,166 --> 00:09:36,000
They’re not always
"slap your thigh" funny.

167
00:09:36,442 --> 00:09:39,312
-Oh.
-Some of them are seriously depressing.

168
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:40,666
Well, that's good.

169
00:09:45,151 --> 00:09:49,355
It would just be the dream for me
to go to Vienna with you

170
00:09:49,466 --> 00:09:51,166
and for you to show me

171
00:09:51,233 --> 00:09:53,900
the world of Vienna,
as I have shown you Torrance.

172
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:55,995
-We should do that.
-I would come there to eat.

173
00:09:56,062 --> 00:09:57,863
-Fine.
-I'm gonna pass on the opera.

174
00:09:57,930 --> 00:09:59,865
-Gonna pass on the architecture.
-Yeah, yeah.

175
00:09:59,933 --> 00:10:01,933
-No, no, no.
-I'm gonna pass on the painting.

176
00:10:02,100 --> 00:10:03,500
-As much as we did today.
-Yeah.

177
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:07,066
I really just want to joke around
with the Viennese comedians

178
00:10:07,133 --> 00:10:09,266
of which you tell me
that culture is vibrant.

179
00:10:09,333 --> 00:10:10,966
I can make that happen.

180
00:10:15,533 --> 00:10:17,199
[waitress] How many pancakes you want?

181
00:10:23,866 --> 00:10:25,966
But there is one aspect

182
00:10:26,033 --> 00:10:28,199
that kind of encompasses all of these.

183
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:30,066
-Encompasses.
-Encompasses, thank you.

184
00:10:30,162 --> 00:10:31,897
-If you’re gonna live here...
-You have to say--

185
00:10:31,964 --> 00:10:33,599
-...you gotta talk like we talk.
-Yeah.

186
00:10:35,334 --> 00:10:37,436
-[Christoph] What is this thing?
-[Jerry] Butter.

187
00:10:37,536 --> 00:10:40,806
-I’d love to know what you think of it.
-I think I know already what I think.

188
00:10:42,266 --> 00:10:43,800
I love butter.

189
00:10:45,711 --> 00:10:47,413
[in French]

190
00:10:50,716 --> 00:10:52,551
Maybe you would like to try a crepe.

191
00:10:52,618 --> 00:10:55,721
-Much more European than a pancake.
-I agree.

192
00:10:55,788 --> 00:10:56,689
Do you have one?

193
00:11:08,434 --> 00:11:09,869
-How is that?
-Mmm.

194
00:11:10,333 --> 00:11:12,033
The great thing about this is,

195
00:11:12,133 --> 00:11:15,766
how they can make it look
so different from what it tastes.

196
00:11:20,633 --> 00:11:23,366
Okay, so, I see here
they have a Belgian waffle.

197
00:11:23,733 --> 00:11:24,666
A what?

198
00:11:24,766 --> 00:11:26,033
Now that’s from Belgium.

199
00:11:27,186 --> 00:11:28,087
Did you know that?

200
00:11:30,233 --> 00:11:31,833
You want to try it? [chuckles]

201
00:11:31,933 --> 00:11:33,233
You’re going to try it.

202
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,466
[Jerry] So the American pancake,

203
00:11:42,699 --> 00:11:44,033
the French crepe,

204
00:11:44,133 --> 00:11:45,566
the Belgian waffle.

205
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,400
Do you feel like
you’ve kind of been on a journey?

206
00:11:48,533 --> 00:11:51,800
I was about to ask what I ever did to you.

207
00:11:53,846 --> 00:11:56,215
[Christoph]
This IHOP, is that how you say it?

208
00:11:56,566 --> 00:11:58,633
-"I hop"?
-How else could you say it?

209
00:11:58,733 --> 00:12:00,900
-"I hope"?
-I never knew what it is.

210
00:12:01,066 --> 00:12:03,866
I actually always thought
it was cheap shoes.

211
00:12:03,956 --> 00:12:06,092
Why would you think it’s shoes,
because of the hopping?

212
00:12:07,426 --> 00:12:10,896
I’m starting to get this Viennese humor.
There’s quite a bit to it.

213
00:12:10,963 --> 00:12:12,732
[inaudible]

214
00:12:14,633 --> 00:12:17,333
Thank you very much for this...

215
00:12:18,333 --> 00:12:19,500
eye-opener.

216
00:12:20,272 --> 00:12:23,075
Well, that’s not the only thing
that’s gonna open up.

217
00:12:24,176 --> 00:12:25,978
[inaudible]

218
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:42,762
Where have I brought you now, Christoph?

219
00:12:42,862 --> 00:12:44,897
Where have I brought you now?

220
00:12:53,772 --> 00:12:57,109
-This feels civilized, doesn’t it?
-I totally agree.

221
00:12:57,233 --> 00:12:58,366
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

222
00:12:58,433 --> 00:12:59,566
Thank you.

223
00:12:59,633 --> 00:13:01,800
What a wonderful way to spend the morning.

224
00:13:01,933 --> 00:13:03,900
Now I’m starting to understand

225
00:13:03,966 --> 00:13:05,599
the Viennese life perspective.

226
00:13:05,966 --> 00:13:07,300
This is how we do it.

227
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:08,699
Very nice.

228
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:20,332
[Jerry] What are you most often
not happy with?

229
00:13:21,733 --> 00:13:23,066
More or less everything.

230
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:26,033
Good answer.

231
00:13:26,338 --> 00:13:29,809
[Christoph] Cigars is one of these things
is a little bit like I feel about comedy.

232
00:13:29,933 --> 00:13:32,966
It has to be exquisite to be good.

233
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:34,800
If it’s any less,

234
00:13:36,048 --> 00:13:37,650
you might as well skip it.

235
00:13:40,133 --> 00:13:41,233
[Jerry] Great line.

236
00:13:49,795 --> 00:13:52,665
So, does everybody in Vienna
have these sensibilities,

237
00:13:52,731 --> 00:13:55,134
these very refined sensibilities?

238
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:57,533
Everybody, without exception.

239
00:13:59,939 --> 00:14:01,440
-Everybody.
-Everybody.

240
00:14:02,007 --> 00:14:04,910
-I can’t tell if you’re joking or not.
-I can’t either.

